Documentation for queue
-----------------------
rev 0.1, 10/18/99:   Initial release.  No behavioral verilog.
rev 0.2, 11/4/99:    Tied FF scan_en and scan_in pins to gnd.


The queue generator is used to derive queue structures made up of
registers.  On a "push" operation, all values in the queue are
written to the next higher queue entry register, and the new input
is written into queue entry 0.  On a pop, no action is performed;
it is assumed that the "head" pointer will be decremented (pointer
logic is not included in this generator).  The output is read from
whichever queue entry "head" points to; it is assumed that only one
bit of the "head" signal can be a 1.  If no bits of the "head" signal
are a 1 (queue empty or queue overflow condition), then the queue
output is forced to 0.



GENERATOR ARGUMENTS:
--------------------
  NBITS:
    Definition:  
      The width of the data stored in the queue.  
    Legal Values:
      1 <= NBITS <= 128
    Default:
      32

  Depth:
    Definition:
      The depth of the queue (number of registers it contains)
    Legal Values:
      1 <= Depth <= 128
    Default:
      8

  OutputSize:
    Definition:
      The drive strength of the final output driver.
    Legal Values:
      C, D, E
    Default:
      C


Signals:
--------
Inputs:

In_H[NBITS-1:0]      : Data input 
Clk[Depth-1:0]       : Clock, one bit per queue entry
Push_H[Depth-1:0]    : Push control signal, one bit per queue entry
Reset_L[Depth-1:0]   : Queue entry reset signal, one bit per entry
Head_H[Depth-1:0]    : Head-of-queue indicator, one bit per queue entry

Outputs:

Out_H[NBITS-1:0]     : Data output (value at head of queue)